d aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Writes short texts about presenting plans for a future activity at work using correct spelling, grammar, cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to the communicative functions for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Understands important and some specific information and aspects of attitude and intention using a range of appropriate language, in written or spoken documents or presentations."}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn6le12ob08re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Writing a Letter"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you write a letter to a friend, you should include:</p> <ul> <li>A casual greeting like <span class=\"italic\">Dear Sally,</span> or <span class=\"italic\">Hey Peter,</span></li> <li>Begin your letter with some general statements.</li> <li>Letters to friends are informal. Use appropriate language and contractions.</li> <li>Think about words you could use to link sentences together like <span class=\"italic\">anyway, so</span> and <span class=\"italic\">by the way</span>.</li> <li> An informal letter can also sound more like a conversation. For example: <span class=\"italic\">Can you believe his behavior? I can't take him anywhere.</span></li> <li>End your letter in an informal way like <span class=\"italic\">Love,</span> or <span class=\"italic\">Cheers,</span> </li></ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "What do you think I should do?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I'll fill you in with the details when I see you next week."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Talking About Past Habits (used to/would)"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> and <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> have rather similar meanings. They are both used to describe something that happened regularly in the past but doesn't happen any longer.</br> However, there is an important difference between <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> and <span class=\"strong\">would</span>. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\">Would</span> is not used unless you know that the time frame is in the past, while <span class=\"strong\">used to</span> does not require this. <br>Read the following example:<br>  <span class=\"italic\">We used to visit India.</span> (correct) <br><br>  However, when <span class=\"strong\">would</span> replaces <span class=\"strong\">used to</span>, the same example becomes ungrammatical:<br><br>  <span class=\"italic\">We would visit India.</span> (not correct)<br> <span class=\"italic\">We would visit India every summer when we were children.</span> (correct)"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "caramelized"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "cooked slowly until brown and sweet"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The caramelized apples were an unexpected treat!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear in a conversation over a meal."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "on your toes"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to maintain your attention and energy in one place"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You always need to be on your toes with Marco."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "spork"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an eating utensil that is both a spoon and a fork"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Marco has a spork in his back pocket."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "unconventional"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not traditional or usual"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: His behavior was a little unconventional."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to swim against the tide"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to not follow others' ideas and beliefs"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I tend to swim against the tide."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to stand out from the crowd"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be different"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I don't want to stand out from the crowd."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "social etiquette"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "behavior one has around others"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Samuel has very poor social etiquette."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud07"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "cold shoulder"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to not pay attention to someone for a reason, to ignore"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Vanessa gave Marco a cold shoulder."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud08"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "appalling"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "terrible, shocking"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: His behavior has been appalling."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud09"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "at one's wits' end"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be upset after trying every possible solution with no success"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I am at my wits' end with Marco."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn6le12ob08aud10"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Write a 10-12 line dialogue between you and a friend."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Have your friend describe the personality and behavior of a person they know. Do they have any bad habits? Are they unconventional?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Give the friend some advice on how to react."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read the dialogue aloud with a partner."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I write a dialogue about personality and behavior."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I give a friend some advice and suggestions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I read a dialogue about behaviors aloud with a partner."}]}]}